{#
  Sets the colour scheme before the first paint, so there is no flash of the wrong theme.

  It must stay inline and stay in <head>: an external file is a round trip, and by the time it
  lands the wrong colours are already on screen. The CSP transform hashes whatever this renders,
  so editing it cannot break the policy.

  `<html>` already carries `dark`, so this only ever needs to take it off.

  The `?theme=` branch is the handoff between tzortzoglou.eu and app.tzortzoglou.eu. localStorage
  is per-origin, so a choice made on one is invisible to the other, and crossing over used to
  flip the site back to dark in your face. The header appends the parameter to its cross-property
  link (see scripts/chrome.js), this adopts it, and this then takes it straight back out of the
  address bar - in <head> rather than on DOMContentLoaded, because consent.js injects GoatCounter
  and GoatCounter records location.search when it loads, which a later cleanup would race.

  Adopted ONLY when this origin has no preference of its own - otherwise arriving from the site
  would overrule a choice deliberately made inside the app. A cookie on .tzortzoglou.eu would
  also have worked and would have been less code; it was rejected because /cookies/ says this
  site sets none, and that claim is worth more than the code it costs.
#}
<script>(function(){var m=/[?&]theme=(light|dark)(&|$)/.exec(location.search);var t=null;try{t=localStorage.getItem('theme');}catch(e){}if(!t&&m){t=m[1];try{localStorage.setItem('theme',t);}catch(e){}}if(t==='light'){document.documentElement.classList.remove('dark');}if(m){try{var u=new URL(location.href);u.searchParams.delete('theme');history.replaceState(null,'',u.pathname+u.search+u.hash);}catch(e){}}})();</script>
